  "content": "In the 14th century, a theological controversy erupted in Byzantium that crystallized something essential about Orthodox spirituality. **Barlaam of Calabria**, a Greek monk educated in the Latin West, criticized the hesychast monks of Mt. Athos who claimed to experience the uncreated light of God in contemplative prayer — the same light witnessed by the disciples at the Transfiguration (Matthew 17:1–8).\\n\\nBarlaam argued this was impossible: God's essence is utterly unknowable and cannot be directly experienced by humans.\\n\\n**Gregory Palamas** (1296–1359) responded with a theological distinction that became foundational for Orthodox thought: God's **essence** (*ousia*) is indeed unknowable and incommunicable — but God's **energies** (*energeiai*) are the genuine self-manifestation of the divine, truly God yet distinct from His essence. Humans can participate in the divine energies through prayer, sacrament, and ascetic life.\\n\\nThis **Palamite theology** was vindicated by a series of councils in Constantinople (1341, 1347, 1351) and remains the Orthodox theological framework for understanding theosis, mystical experience, and the nature of grace. It has no direct parallel in Catholic or Protestant theology, and represents one of the most distinctive Orthodox contributions to Christian thought."
